  "decisions": [
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-1016-j-pragma-2010-04-011",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "The abstract gives useful empirical characterization of question and response types, but only at the question-response sequence level. It does not show question-to-span alignment, decomposition of multi-question messages, proposition-level answer scope, or identification of which subquestions remain unresolved. The more operational coding-scheme and multi-unit-question candidates admitted below strengthen the relevant streams more directly.",
      "confidence": 0.91
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-1145-3209978-3210046",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "F3. The abstract directly studies a large enterprise-email collection, defines extraction of question-answer pairs from threaded emails, and evaluates question-to-answer matching at multiple granularities on manually labeled email data. This is unusually close to msgloom's target domain and directly strengthens gap-3 by supplying empirical evidence for linking naturally occurring workplace-email questions to responding spans. Identity is UNCHECKED, but no VERIFIED candidate supplied here offers comparable enterprise-email QA-alignment evidence.",
      "stream_id": "F3",
      "role": "DIRECT",
      "confidence": 0.99
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-2307-412243",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "The work is foundational for conversational sequencing, but the supplied abstract does not provide proposition-level antecedent annotations, response-target identification, question-answer alignment, or scope judgments. Its turn-taking concepts are too indirect for the present reading round.",
      "confidence": 0.98
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "manual-849c5f1c6d",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "F4. The abstract explicitly describes authentic customer-support email where one incoming message may contain several questions, identifies portions of the customer query that require responses, and learns associations between actual questions and answers from query-response email pairs. That is direct empirical evidence for decomposing multi-question email and matching response material to the parts requiring answers, making it highly relevant to gap-3. Identity is UNCHECKED, but its multi-question email evidence is not duplicated by a VERIFIED candidate in this set.",
      "stream_id": "F4",
      "role": "DIRECT",
      "confidence": 0.96
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-3115-981623-981630",
      "decision": "PENDING",
      "reason": "The abstract establishes discourse-anaphoric reference in conversation but does not make clear whether the antecedents are specifically clauses, propositions, events, facts, or other non-nominal spans rather than ordinary discourse entities. Admission would require full-text evidence that the analysis explicitly identifies non-nominal antecedent spans or abstract discourse referents of the kind required by F1.",
      "confidence": 0.84
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-1016-j-pragma-2010-04-002",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "F4. The abstract presents an empirical annotation scheme for naturally occurring question-response sequences that distinguishes answers, non-answer responses, and absence of a response, while separately coding confirmation and disconfirmation for polar questions. It therefore supplies directly reusable empirical distinctions for unresolved versus responded-to questions and is relevant to gap-3 and the annotation side of gap-2. It does not by itself solve multi-question proposition scope. Identity is UNCHECKED, but no VERIFIED candidate here provides the same explicit answer/non-answer/no-response coding scheme.",
      "stream_id": "F4",
      "role": "DIRECT",
      "confidence": 0.91
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-5087-dad-2020-104",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "F6. The abstract explicitly studies complete SDRT structures in situated multi-party chat, including discourse threads, nonlinguistic events, higher-order structures, and targets that can be complex discourse units rather than a single preceding utterance. This is direct evidence for representing composite discourse antecedents and materially strengthens gap-4. Identity is UNCHECKED, but the higher-order/composite-target capability is distinctive within the candidate set.",
      "stream_id": "F6",
      "role": "DIRECT",
      "confidence": 0.96
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-3389-frobt-2023-949600",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "The abstract clearly supports ordinary F2 antecedent-link and relation prediction, but it does not show partial reply scope, multiple antecedents, composite targets, ambiguity/no-link treatment, or multi-question answer resolution. Its core attachment capability is already represented more directly in this reading round by the VERIFIED structured dialogue parser 2306.15103, so this additional domain/model variant does not justify another slot.",
      "confidence": 0.91
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "2110.04526",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "Although VERIFIED and clearly relevant to F2, the abstract is primarily about cross-domain improvement of ordinary discourse attachment and relation prediction. It does not add evidence for partial proposition scope, multi-antecedent responses, unanswered subquestions, or ambiguity/no-link behavior. For this gap-focused round, the F2 capability is sufficiently represented by the admitted VERIFIED structured parser 2306.15103.",
      "confidence": 0.91
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-1177-1367006912441419",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "The abstract shows how reformulating an open question into a yes-no question or alternatives changes the recipient's response space, but it does not show annotation or prediction of which prior subquestion a response answers, whether only part is answered, or which parts remain unresolved. The phenomenon is relevant background but does not materially supply the target capability.",
      "confidence": 0.89
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "manual-a9d613628b",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "DAMSL's backward communicative functions are relevant relation labels, including answer, acceptance, and confirmation, but the supplied abstract does not establish explicit proposition/span target pointers or scope boundaries. Multiple dialogue-act functions on one utterance are not evidence of multiple antecedents. For Topic 04 this is therefore primarily speech-act labeling without demonstrated antecedent scope, one of the stated false friends.",
      "confidence": 0.93
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-1515-text-2003-021",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "F4. The abstract reports a substantial empirical corpus of multi-unit questions from authentic institutional interactions and analyzes how several interrogative and declarative units jointly constrain the subsequent response. This directly strengthens the empirical basis for reasoning about multi-part question structures and answer scope, which is central to gap-3. The abstract does not establish exact automatic span alignment, so its contribution is empirical rather than a complete engineering solution. Identity is UNCHECKED.",
      "stream_id": "F4",
      "role": "DIRECT",
      "confidence": 0.92
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-3115-1118121-1118124",
      "decision": "PENDING",
      "reason": "The abstract establishes that short and non-sentential responses require contextual antecedents, but it does not say whether the corpus explicitly annotates or evaluates antecedent selection, whether antecedents are proposition-level/non-nominal, or whether ambiguity and no-link cases are represented. Full text would need to show explicit antecedent representations or evaluated resolution relevant to F1 or F10.",
      "confidence": 0.82
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "1812.00176",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "This VERIFIED paper directly implements F2 by selecting a previous EDU and predicting its discourse relation, but the abstract describes the standard single-antecedent dialogue-dependency problem and provides no evidence for partial response scope, multiple/composite antecedents, unresolved questions, or abstention. Its basic F2 contribution is duplicated in this reading round by the admitted VERIFIED structured parser 2306.15103.",
      "confidence": 0.94
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-18653-v1-d19-1234",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "The abstract concerns weak supervision for ordinary dialogue discourse attachment. It does not show the partial, ambiguous, multi-antecedent, no-link, or multi-question phenomena needed by the current gaps, and the underlying F2 attachment capability is already covered by an admitted VERIFIED paper.",
      "confidence": 0.94
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-24963-ijcai-2021-543",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "The joint edge-modeling approach materially concerns F2 discourse parsing, but the supplied abstract does not show multiple antecedents for one response, composite proposition targets, partial reply scope, or unanswered-question handling. It is another modeling variant for an already represented F2 capability rather than evidence that closes the current scope gaps.",
      "confidence": 0.93
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-15760-mcnair-2019-13-1-2",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "F1. The abstract explicitly states that the corpus annotation marks non-nominal antecedents of it, this, and that when they refer to events, facts, propositions, or other abstract discourse entities, and includes spontaneous dialogue among the compared registers. That is directly aligned with Topic 04's distinction between entity coreference and propositional/discourse reference. Identity is UNCHECKED, but the abstract shows the required antecedent ontology unambiguously.",
      "stream_id": "F1",
      "role": "DIRECT",
      "confidence": 0.97
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "2306.15103",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "F2. This VERIFIED paper jointly predicts discourse links and relation types in multi-participant conversation and explicitly supports labeled, multi-root, non-projective structures and nonlocal dependencies. It provides a strong modern reference implementation/evaluation point for antecedent attachment plus relation classification. It does not by itself establish partial or multi-antecedent reply scope, so it is admitted for F2 rather than as closure of gap-4.",
      "stream_id": "F2",
      "role": "DIRECT",
      "confidence": 0.96
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "manual-e4d6ad7ca1",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "F6. The abstract explicitly says ARRAU represents multiple antecedents for ambiguous anaphoric expressions and discourse antecedents for abstract entities including events, actions, and plans, with task-oriented TRAINS dialogues in the corpus. This directly strengthens gap-4 and supplies important annotation-design evidence for gap-2 because it preserves ambiguity and multiple antecedents rather than forcing one link. Identity is UNCHECKED, but no VERIFIED candidate here exposes the same combination of multiple antecedents, abstract discourse reference, and dialogue annotation.",
      "stream_id": "F6",
      "role": "DIRECT",
      "confidence": 0.98
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-18653-v1-2021-codi-sharedtask-3",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "The discourse-deixis component is directly relevant to F1, but this is a system description for a shared task. The admitted CODI-CRAC 2022 shared-task overview is more useful for this round because it exposes the conversational datasets, annotation task, and evaluation regime, while the admitted abstract-anaphora corpus provides complementary antecedent evidence. The supplied abstract does not show an additional scope or annotation capability that warrants another reading slot.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-3115-1219840-1219869",
      "decision": "PENDING",
      "reason": "The abstract indicates that non-sentential utterances in multilogue may resolve to non-adjacent contextual antecedents, which could strengthen F10. It does not establish whether antecedent candidates are explicitly annotated or evaluated, whether absent/ambiguous alternatives are preserved, or whether the antecedents are proposition-level. Full text would need to demonstrate one of those capabilities before admission.",
      "confidence": 0.8
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "manual-b0133009f6",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "F1. The abstract describes conversational datasets and explicit evaluation tasks for discourse deixis in dialogue, where referring expressions must be linked to antecedent discourse content that can be propositional or otherwise non-nominal. This is directly useful to F1 and provides modern annotation/evaluation evidence relevant to gap-2. Identity is UNCHECKED, but the shared-task benchmark contribution is distinct from the linguistic corpus evidence supplied by the other admitted F1 paper.",
      "stream_id": "F1",
      "role": "DIRECT",
      "confidence": 0.96
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-18653-v1-2024-codi-1-15",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "The abstract provides F2 attachment and relation prediction, including evaluation on technical chat, but it does not show partial response scope, multi-antecedent/composite targets, unresolved subquestions, or explicit no-link/ambiguity outcomes. Because the core F2 capability is already represented by VERIFIED 2306.15103, and this candidate is UNCHECKED, the verified paper is preferred for the reading slot.",
      "confidence": 0.93
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "manual-9571289efd",
      "decision": "PENDING",
      "reason": "No abstract was supplied. Admission requires evidence that the paper analyzes demonstratives whose antecedents are clauses, propositions, events, facts, situations, or other non-nominal discourse spans, rather than merely ordinary nominal demonstrative reference.",
      "confidence": 0.99
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-3115-981344-981353",
      "decision": "PENDING",
      "reason": "The abstract shows that demonstratives can select discourse entities outside the current local center, but it does not say whether those discourse entities are non-nominal propositions/events or ordinary nominal referents. Full text would need to establish abstract/propositional antecedent cases or an evaluated antecedent-selection scheme relevant to F1 or F10.",
      "confidence": 0.84
    }
  ],
  "notes": "On the supplied abstracts, the strongest admissions are the enterprise-email question-answer study and multi-question customer-email study for gap-3; the situated-discourse and ARRAU work for composite/multiple antecedents and gap-4; and the abstract-anaphora/CODI-CRAC resources for non-nominal antecedent annotation and evaluation. The admitted question-response coding scheme and ARRAU annotations provide useful components for gap-2, but no candidate abstract validates the full msgloom contract combining exact, partial, ambiguous, unsupported, discontinuous, multiple-antecedent, and explicit no-link outcomes. Gap-1 remains open: none of the supplied abstracts directly evaluates proposition-level approval, agreement, rejection, or clarification scope in authentic workplace email or enterprise chat. Gap-5 also remains open: none establishes confidence thresholds or asymmetric error costs for abstention versus over-broad response links."
